"A sponge can absorb a lot of water.": "1fdf9904e824",
"An ink stick looks like a small black bar, but it is made from soot. Makers burn pine wood or oil, collect the fine black soot, mix it with glue, and press it into molds to dry. To make liquid ink, you put a few drops of water on the inkstone and rub the stick in slow circles. Bit by bit, the soot comes off, and the water turns black. Grinding takes time, so many people today use bottled ink, but grinding is also a calm way to get ready to write.": "b5d182dccd1d",
"Calligraphers often write on Xuan paper, which is named after Xuanzhou in Anhui Province, China. It is made by hand from the bark of the blue sandalwood tree and rice straw. Raw Xuan paper drinks in water, so a drop of ink spreads out with soft edges, and the paper shows every move of the brush. Copy paper is different. It is sized, or coated, so the water cannot soak in, and the ink sits on top. In Taiwan, the town of Puli has made handmade paper since the Japanese era.": "81c47cdca87c",
"Calligraphy is not only for experts or for grandparents. In Taiwan, students begin writing with a brush in third or fourth grade, and many people write spring couplets for the Lunar New Year. You do not even need ink to start: a brush dipped in clean water can practice on old newspaper or on a water-writing cloth, where the marks fade as they dry. In this lesson, first watch the 3D brush write the character 一 (one) in slow motion. Then try writing it yourself on the practice pad.": "140fca60ab98",
"Calligraphy is the art of beautiful writing. In Chinese calligraphy, people write characters with a soft brush and black ink, and they need only four tools: a brush, an ink stick, paper, and an inkstone. Together these are called the Four Treasures of the Study, because for centuries they sat on the desk in a scholar's study. People have written with brushes for a very long time. The oldest brush ever found came from a tomb near Changsha and is more than 2,200 years old.": "728e3691897f",
"Calligraphy is the art of beautiful writing. In Chinese calligraphy, people write characters with a soft brush and black ink, and they need only four tools: a brush, an ink stick, paper, and an inkstone. Together these are called the Four Treasures of the Study, because for centuries they sat on the desk in a scholar's study. People have written with brushes for a very long time. The oldest brush ever found came from a tomb near Changsha and is more than 2,200 years old. The brush is a bundle of animal hair tied into a point and fixed to a bamboo handle. Soft white goat hair holds a lot of ink and makes round, gentle lines. Springy brown hair from the tail of a weasel makes sharp, strong lines. In Chinese it is called “wolf hair,” but it usually comes from a weasel. Many brushes mix the two kinds. A good brush has four virtues: its tip is sharp, its hairs are even, its body is round, and it springs back after you press it. An ink stick looks like a small black bar, but it is made from soot. Makers burn pine wood or oil, collect the fine black soot, mix it with glue, and press it into molds to dry. To make liquid ink, you put a few drops of water on the inkstone and rub the stick in slow circles. Bit by bit, the soot comes off, and the water turns black. Grinding takes time, so many people today use bottled ink, but grinding is also a calm way to get ready to write. Calligraphers often write on Xuan paper, which is named after Xuanzhou in Anhui Province, China. It is made by hand from the bark of the blue sandalwood tree and rice straw. Raw Xuan paper drinks in water, so a drop of ink spreads out with soft edges, and the paper shows every move of the brush. Copy paper is different. It is sized, or coated, so the water cannot soak in, and the ink sits on top. In Taiwan, the town of Puli has made handmade paper since the Japanese era. The inkstone is a flat piece of fine, hard stone. Its surface is smooth but a little rough, just enough to grind the ink stick quickly. The flat part where you grind is called the hill, and the deep part at one end is called the sea, where the finished ink collects. Some of the most famous inkstones are Duan inkstones, carved from purple stone near Zhaoqing in Guangdong Province, an area that was called Duanzhou in the Tang dynasty. Calligraphy is not only for experts or for grandparents. In Taiwan, students begin writing with a brush in third or fourth grade, and many people write spring couplets for the Lunar New Year. You do not even need ink to start: a brush dipped in clean water can practice on old newspaper or on a water-writing cloth, where the marks fade as they dry. In this lesson, first watch the 3D brush write the character 一 (one) in slow motion. Then try writing it yourself on the practice pad.": "1173523d0718",
